    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d239">

                <pb facs="0266" n="150" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg150"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.51/1051.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the
                            Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-09-11">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 11, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Chinese Government is reported to have signed a preliminary agreement for a
                    loan of £10,000,000 secured on the salt gabelle, with Lloyd’s Bank and Union of
                    London and Smith’s Bank.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d239-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Calhoun.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d239-f1" n="2" xml:id="d239-f1-content">
                <p>This is later known as “the Crisp loan” (C. Birch Crisp &amp; Co.); references to
                    it occur at <ref target="#pg152">pp. 152</ref>, <ref target="#pg153">153</ref>
                    and later; officially it was “The Chinese Government 5 per cent Gold Loan of
                    1912.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
